n between.\n\nOf those 228:\n\n| | Count | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| *Re: Long thread* | **200** | one synthetic 200-message thread, `<edge-thread-200-0@edge.test>` |\n| MIME / encoding / image / attachment torture cases | 25 | `att-pkpass-zip-bomb`, `enc-rfc2047-broken`, `mime-boundary-absent`, `img-1x1`, `att-pdf-2000-pages`… |\n| Header and date oddities | 13 | `date-year-9999` (dated 31 Dec 9999), `date-epoch` (1 Jan 1970), `garbage headers`, two with no subject at all |\n\nSo the honest number is **228 unread, of which 228 are test fixtures**. A real \"unread count\" for your actual work is **zero** — the newest unread is seven and a half years old and comes from a sender called \"Edge Sender\".\n\nTwo things this shares with your mail elsewhere: your inbox''s newest message is still **26 September 2026**, and one connected mailbox is all this workspace holds, so there''s no second inbox where real unread might be hiding.\n\nWorth knowing: `date-year-9999` sits at the top of any date-sorted view, which is why an unread notification for it could look like something new. It isn''t.\n\nWant me to mark the whole 2024 test corpus read, or archive it out of the inbox? Either is one action, and I''d scope it to the `@edge.test` senders so nothing real is touched."}','assistant','v2','2026-11-22T04:32:59.811Z','f0c4be98-b2fd-59d7-b11e-c921e7d22615');
